"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. Preoperative PSA was elevated at 7.93. The surgical findings included a Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2), pT2 stage, positive surgical margins, and no seminal vesicle invasion or lymphovascular invasion. The absence of lymph node removal and missing nodal sampling widen uncertainty regarding nodal status. The difference between the biopsy and prostatectomy findings is noted, as the biopsy Gleason pattern and ISUP report are missing.\n\nCountervailing factors include the favorable surgical findings regarding local extension and seminal vesicles. The postoperative PSA trajectory and subsequent follow-up are unknown, which is a significant factor in determining the long-term outcome. Information regarding postoperative PSA levels and their subsequent trajectory would be crucial to revise this prognosis. The current horizon suggests a follow-up period of approximately 63 months without an observed event. The fixed estimated horizon is 63.30 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 41.14–85.45 months; its coverage has not been validated."
